    "chunk_text": "Aerospace Engineers in the Indian space sector commonly correspond to roles such as ISRO Scientist or ISRO Engineer working within the Indian Space Research Organisation. These professionals design, test, and operate spacecraft systems, launch vehicles, satellites, and mission technologies that support India\u2019s space exploration, communication, and remote sensing programs. Core daily responsibilities include designing spacecraft components and propulsion systems, conducting simulation and testing of satellite hardware, analyzing launch data and mission performance, coordinating multidisciplinary engineering teams during mission development, and maintaining or upgrading satellite systems after deployment. Essential skills include systems engineering, analytical thinking, mathematics, programming, problem solving, teamwork, research ability, technical communication, electronics design, and project management. Important knowledge areas include aerospace engineering, physics, orbital mechanics, electronics and control systems, and advanced mathematics. Work style emphasizes precision, innovation, and persistence, suiting investigative and realistic personalities who enjoy solving complex scientific and engineering problems. In India entry typically occurs through ISRO Centralised Recruitment Board examinations or campus hiring from top engineering institutes such as IITs, IISc, NITs, and other strong engineering colleges, usually requiring a B.Tech or often an M.Tech in aerospace, electronics, mechanical, or related fields. Fresh recruits earn roughly 10\u201312 LPA including allowances, with mid-career scientists earning around 18\u201325 LPA and senior mission directors exceeding 35 LPA equivalents with strong job stability and national prestige. Work is concentrated in Bengaluru, Sriharikota, Thiruvananthapuram, and Hyderabad. Internationally similar careers exist in organizations such as NASA, ESA, and private space companies, with strong demand for experienced aerospace engineers globally.",
