    "chunk_text": "Agricultural and Horticultural Specialists in India commonly include Agriculture Officers and Horticulturists working in state agriculture departments, research institutions, and agribusiness companies. These professionals support farmers, develop crop improvement strategies, manage agricultural extension programs, and promote sustainable farming practices across rural regions. Typical daily tasks include advising farmers on crop management and fertilizers, conducting field inspections and soil testing, implementing government agriculture schemes, analyzing crop productivity data, and coordinating training programs for rural farming communities. Essential skills include agronomy knowledge, communication with rural communities, problem solving, data analysis, environmental awareness, crop management, research skills, planning, and technical advisory abilities. Knowledge areas include plant science, soil science, agricultural economics, pest management, and irrigation systems. Work style emphasizes field engagement, service orientation, and analytical observation, fitting realistic and social personalities interested in agriculture and rural development. In India entry often occurs through state Agriculture Officer recruitment exams or through qualifications such as B.Sc Agriculture from ICAR-accredited universities. Entry salaries generally range from 6\u20139 LPA with growth to 12\u201320 LPA in senior roles within government departments or agribusiness firms. Work opportunities are distributed across rural districts, state capitals, and agricultural research centers. Internationally agricultural expertise is valuable in global food systems, agritech companies, and environmental sustainability initiatives.",
