    "extractedSkillsCount": 10,
    "description": "",
    "chunk_text": "Government Administrative Clerks and Public Sector Banking Officers in India include roles such as SSC CGL officers, SSC CHSL clerical staff, Bank Probationary Officers (PO), and Bank Clerks recruited through IBPS and SBI examinations. These professionals support the day-to-day functioning of government departments and public sector banks by processing documentation, managing financial records, handling customer or citizen requests, maintaining databases, and assisting in administrative decision processes. Typical tasks include verifying documents, processing government forms or banking transactions, maintaining financial ledgers, responding to public queries, coordinating with other departments, and ensuring regulatory compliance in daily operations. Key skills include attention to detail, numerical reasoning, communication, documentation management, customer service, data entry accuracy, basic financial literacy, problem solving, administrative coordination, and computer proficiency. Important knowledge areas include banking operations, government procedures, accounting basics, office management, and regulatory compliance. Work style emphasizes reliability, organization, and service orientation, fitting conventional and social personality types who prefer structured work environments with clear procedures. Entry into these roles typically occurs through exams such as SSC CGL, SSC CHSL, IBPS PO, IBPS Clerk, or SBI PO after completing a bachelor\u2019s degree or sometimes 12th pass for certain clerical roles. Starting salaries range from about 4\u20138 LPA depending on role and allowances, rising to 10\u201318 LPA in senior managerial banking positions or higher grade government administrative posts. These jobs are widely distributed across Indian cities and towns, offering strong stability, predictable career progression, and highly valued government job security within Indian society.",
